A||N||8||Bugsy||Dead on the road||||8||
Z||000000||Bugsy||02-26-2000||08:30 AM||||Ok, one minute my car was running great like normal then like someone hit a switch it died. I got it started before it rolled to a stop. and it ran like usual...for about 1/2 mile and it died again. I know its getting gas, but I haven't had time to dig any deeper than that, I question whether it is getting any spark. Any sugestions as to where to start? TIA for any input<BR>Kevin ||||reg||8
Z||000001||Oreif||02-26-2000||10:53 AM||||Is your car a V-6? If it is you should check out the pick-up coil in the distributor.  They go out often. That's what happen to my car. I was driving along and it just shut down!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ed||02-26-2000||01:11 PM||||I agree with Oreif.  The ignition module in your distributor will INSTANTLY die. <P>I was cruising along in the mountains by Lake Tahoe a couple of years ago, marveling at how well the car was running, when POOF, it died dead, and I mean DEAD. No missing, no nothing, dead. When the tow truck came we fooled around with it a little. We cranked it, smelled gas in the exhaust pipe, so that was okay.  We tried the time-tested method of looking for spark: none. I had it towed home, went to the parts house where they tested the module, and sure enough it was gone. <P>The general recommendation is to replace electrical parts such as modules with OEM. Supposedly they're better than the parts house ones. And the one in your car now is easily removed to be tested. <p>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 02-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||02-26-2000||05:32 PM||||It's probably something electrical like the ignition module or a fusible link.  You said you're sure you're getting gas - but are you sure you're getting enough gas?<P>The fuel pump on my 84 died a sudden death and produced symptoms like yours.  I could hear it weakly buzzing away as my car stumbled and sputtered.  Then the pump quit buzzing and the car died for good.  Had a nice walk in the rain to call a two truck.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>Turn your ignition to ON (not all the way to START) and prick up your ears (insert joke here).  The fuel pump should buzz strongly for about 3-5 sec, then shut off.  If it doesn't, it's probably bad.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bugsy||02-26-2000||05:43 PM||||Sorry guys i was a bit distraut this mornin.<BR>its an 86SE v-6. i know its getting gas because the poor guy standing behind the car the last time i tried to start is got gassed out...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bugsy||02-27-2000||07:21 PM||||Well I pulled the distributor cap...and discovered it was original, what a mess, more carbon tracks than i've ever seen in a cap, the rotor was fried...also original i'm sure, and the ignition module tested bad as well, and this thing ran good till it quit. its amazing it ran at all, it has 80,000 miles on it. Anyway, i put it all back together, sat in the drivers seat for like 10 minutes before i had the guts to try it. It was running almost before i hit the starter...WOW what a difference, its like a totally different motor.<BR>Thanx for the input guys, <BR>Kevin<P>[This message has been edited by Bugsy (edited 02-27-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Bugsy (edited 02-27-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000006||BENGAL4||02-27-2000||08:04 PM||||Glad to see you fixed the problem. I carry a spare ignition module in a plastic bag in my trunk. I was left stranded once, but never again. Those things quit with no warning.||||reg||14
Z||000007||Ed||02-27-2000||11:47 PM||||I pack a spare module around also after my problem (see above). <P>Bugsy, seeing your fix reminded me that my prior Fiero, a four, died suddenly at an intersection. Same problem, bad distributor and rotor.  So I've had two sudden deaths: Module in the six and distributor and rotor in the four. <P>In the old, pre-computer days cars usually ran badly for a while, then quit.  Now they run a lot better most of the time until they sometimes just flat out quit without warning.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||stsmithgt||02-28-2000||03:58 PM||||That's sooo fun to be ripping across an intersection then -------nothing.  That's happened twice to me.  I want something better than stock.  Doesn't Accel make a high temperature pickup coil.  Is it any good?  Does it last longer than a year?  This annual change got expensive when I didn't know what was going on.  Now I know of the all magic Pennock Forum! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  This place is like asking the crystal ball a question.  I'm glad so many people contribute their 2 cents.  Thanks||||reg||1
